import Mathlib
/-! QED-E206: Is strong shift equivalence of nonnegative integer matrices decidable?
Call two square matrices A and B with nonnegative integer entries elementarily equivalent if A = RS and B = SR for
some rectangular nonnegative integer matrices R and S, and strongly shift equivalent if they are linked by a finite
chain of such moves. By Williams' 1973 theorem this is exactly when two shifts of finite type are topologically
conjugate. Is there an algorithm that decides, given A and B, whether they are strongly shift equivalent?
Informal statement: For $A\in\mathbb Z_{\ge0}^{m\times m}$, $B\in\mathbb Z_{\ge0}^{n\times n}$ ($m,n\ge1$) write
$A\approx B$ if there are $R\in\mathbb Z_{\ge0}^{m\times n}$, $S\in\mathbb Z_{\ge0}^{n\times m}$ with $A=RS$,
$B=SR$; let $\sim_{SSE}$ be the equivalence relation generated by $\approx$ on square matrices of all positive
sizes. Determine whether $\{(A,B):A\sim_{SSE}B\}$ is a computable set (it is computably enumerable).
Formalization notes: A square matrix of size m+1 (m : ℕ) is an element of `SqMat = Σ m, Matrix (Fin (m+1))
(Fin (m+1)) ℕ`; sizes are ≥ 1 as in the literature (0 × 0 matrices are excluded so that zero matrices are not
artificially linked through the empty matrix). `Elem A B` is the elementary move A = RS, B = SR with R, S
rectangular over ℕ. SSE is the reflexive–transitive closure of the symmetric closure of `Elem`, i.e. the
equivalence relation generated by it. Inputs are codes (m, l) : ℕ × List ℕ, decoded as the (m+1) × (m+1) matrix
whose (i, j) entry is the (i(m+1)+j)-th element of l (0 if l is too short; extra entries are ignored). Decoding is
computable and surjective, so decidability of SSE on codes is decidability of SSE on matrices. "Computable set" is
Mathlib's `ComputablePred` on the Primcodable type of code pairs. `Claim` = SSE over ℤ₊ is decidable;
`¬Claim` = it is undecidable.
-/
namespace OpenProblems.QEDE206

noncomputable section

/-- Square nonnegative integer matrices of all positive sizes. -/
abbrev SqMat : Type := Σ m : ℕ, Matrix (Fin (m + 1)) (Fin (m + 1)) ℕ

/-- Elementary strong shift equivalence: A = RS and B = SR. -/
def Elem (A B : SqMat) : Prop :=
  ∃ R : Matrix (Fin (A.1 + 1)) (Fin (B.1 + 1)) ℕ, ∃ S : Matrix (Fin (B.1 + 1)) (Fin (A.1 + 1)) ℕ,
    A.2 = R * S ∧ B.2 = S * R

/-- Strong shift equivalence: the equivalence relation generated by `Elem`. -/
def SSE (A B : SqMat) : Prop :=
  Relation.ReflTransGen (fun X Y : SqMat => Elem X Y ∨ Elem Y X) A B

/-- Decode (m, l) as the (m+1)×(m+1) matrix with row-major entries from l (missing entries 0). -/
def decode (c : ℕ × List ℕ) : SqMat :=
  ⟨c.1, Matrix.of fun i j : Fin (c.1 + 1) => c.2.getD (i.val * (c.1 + 1) + j.val) 0⟩

def Claim : Prop :=
  ComputablePred (fun p : (ℕ × List ℕ) × (ℕ × List ℕ) => SSE (decode p.1) (decode p.2))

end

end OpenProblems.QEDE206
